{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Complications after rectal resection are frequent. Recently, methods to assess visceral obesity (VO) have become available as an alternative to measurement of body mass index (BMI). The aim of this study was to examine the association between visceral fat volume (VFV) and the short-term outcomes after laparoscopic low anterior resection (LLAR) in patients with rectal cancer. METHODS: We studied a consecutive series of patients undergoing LLAR at Bispebjerg University Hospital from 01.01.2013 to 01.01.2016. Preoperative VFV was calculated from abdominal CT scans using an automatic segmentation tool. The primary outcome was anastomotic leakage (AL). Secondary outcomes included conversion to open surgery, number of lymph nodes harvested, the rates of 30-day complications as well as reoperations, and 1-year survival. RESULTS: A total of 102 patients were included. VO was defined as a VFV above the 75 percentile. Thirteen (12.7%) patients developed AL, four (15.4%) of whom were in the VO group (p = 0.900). At least one postoperative complication developed in 38 (37.3%) patients, with no significant difference between the VO and non-VO patients after univariable analysis (42.3% vs. 35.4%, p = 0.702) or multivariable adjustment (OR 1.01, 95% CI 0.38-2.65, p = 0.984). VO was significantly associated with an increased incidence of conversion to open surgery (OR 4.30, 95% CI 1.29-14.86, p = 0.018). There was a significant difference in the number of harvested lymph nodes between the two groups (mean 23.5 vs. 29.1, p = 0.045). CONCLUSIONS: In this study on patients undergoing laparoscopic rectal resection, VO was not associated with development of AL or other complications. However, we found that visceral obesity was associated with an increased risk of conversion to open surgery.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Baastrup, Niklas Nygaard","Christensen, Jan Kim","Jensen, Kristian Kiim","Jørgensen, Lars Nannestad"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-397988"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Body Mass Index","Conversion to Open Surgery statistics numerical data","Female","Humans","Incidence","Intra Abdominal Fat pathology","Laparoscopy adverse effects","Male","Middle Aged","Obesity Abdominal diagnosis","Organ Size","Postoperative Complications diagnosis","Proctectomy adverse effects","Rectal Neoplasms pathology","Retrospective Studies","Risk Adjustment methods"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"visceral obesity and short term outcomes after laparoscopic rectal cancer resection","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-397988","identifier":[{"id":"30887182","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85063109134","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-078deb49-0f91-4bb9-b031-d3e65211ed1e"],"keyword":["Anastomotic Leak/epidemiology","Body Mass Index","Conversion to Open Surgery/statistics & numerical data","Female","Humans","Incidence","Intra-Abdominal Fat/pathology","Laparoscopy/adverse effects","Male","Middle Aged","Obesity, Abdominal/diagnosis","Organ Size","Postoperative Complications/diagnosis","Proctectomy/adverse effects","Rectal Neoplasms/pathology","Retrospective Studies","Risk Adjustment/methods"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-768B4E99C3BCA46DC5509C8041BE3131"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Surgery"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Niklas Nygaard","key":"baastruppau","last":"Baastrup","name":"Baastrup, Niklas Nygaard","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jan Kim","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Jan Kim","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Kristian Kiim","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","name":"Jensen, Kristian Kiim","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lars Nannestad","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"ae2a79a1-5374-4e92-84c9-3af55ef5712e","name":"Jørgensen, Lars Nannestad","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["30887182"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00464-019-06748-4","issn":["09302794","14322218"],"issn_org":["09302794"],"journal":"Surgical Endoscopy","pages":"177-185","status":"p","title":"Surgical Endoscopy","volume":"34"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85063109134"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-078deb49-0f91-4bb9-b031-d3e65211ed1e"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Visceral obesity and short-term outcomes after laparoscopic rectal cancer resection","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-397988","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Purpose: To examine the exercise intensity and impact of 12 months of twice-weekly recreational football training on cardiorespiratory fitness (CRF), blood pressure (BP), resting heart rate (HRrest), body fat mass, blood lipids, inflammation, and health-related quality of life in women treated for early-stage breast cancer (BC). Methods: Sixty-eight women who had received surgery for stage I-III BC and completed adjuvant chemo- and/or radiation therapy within 5 years were randomized in a 2:1 ratio to a Football Fitness group (FFG, n = 46) or a control group (CON, n = 22). Football Fitness sessions comprised a warm-up, drills and 3–4 × 7 min of small-sided games (SSG). Assessments were performed at baseline, 6 months and 12 months. Outcomes were peak oxygen uptake (VO2peak), blood pressure (BP), HRrest, total body fat mass, and circulating plasma lipids and hs-CRP, and the 36-Item Short Form Health Survey (SF36). Intention-to-treat (ITT) analyses were performed using linear mixed models. Data are means with SD or 95% confidence intervals. Results: Adherence to training in participants completing the 12-months follow-up (n = 33) was 47.1% (22.7), and HR during SSG was ≥80% of HRmax for 69.8% (26.5) of total playing time. At baseline, VO2peak was 28.5 (6.4) and 25.6 (5.9) ml O2/kg/min in FFG and CON, respectively, and no significant changes were observed at 6- or 12 months follow-up. Systolic BP (SBP) was 117.1 (16.4) and 116.9 (14.8) mmHg, and diastolic BP (DBP) was 72.0 (11.2) and 72.4 (8.5) mmHg in FFG and CON, respectively, at baseline, and a 9.4 mmHg decrease in SBP in CON at 12 months resulted in a between-group difference at 12 months of 8.7 mmHg (p =.012). Blood lipids and hs-CRP were within the normal range at baseline, and there were no differences in changes between groups over the 12 months. Similarly, no differences between groups were observed in HRrest and body fat mass at 6- and12-months follow-up. A between-group difference in mean changes of 23.5 (0.95–46.11) points in the role-physical domain of the SF36 survey favored FFG at 6 months. Conclusion: Football Fitness training is an intense exercise form for women treated for breast cancer, and self-perceived health-related limitations on daily activities were improved after 6 months. However, 1 year of Football Fitness training comprising 1 weekly training session on average did not improve CRF, BP, blood lipids, fat mass, or HRrest. Trial registration number: The trial was registered at ClinicalTrials.gov with identifier NCT03284567.","author_count":13,"contributors":["Uth, Jacob","Fristrup, Bjørn","Sørensen, Victor","Helge, Eva Wulff","Christensen, Maja Kjærgaard","Kjærgaard, Julie Boye","Møller, Trine Kjeldgaard","Mohr, Magni","Helge, Jørn Wulff","Jørgensen, Niklas Rye","Rørth, Mikael","Vadstrup, Eva Soelberg","Krustrup, Peter"],"cuid":["S03-099.94-018333"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["Asia, East","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Uth, Jacob"],"country":["China","Faroe Islands","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Shanghai University of Sport","University of Exeter","University of the Faroe Islands"],"keyword":["Blood Pressure","Blood lipids","Breast cancer rehabilitation","Faculty of Science","Fitness","Soccer","VO max","VO2max"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"exercise intensity and cardiovascular health outcomes after 000000000012 months of football fitness training in women treated for stage i iii breast cancer results from the football fitness after breast cancer abc randomized controlled trial","src":["au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85089827219","version":"Other Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["JU and PK developed the study concept and initiated the project together with MR and ESV. EWH, JWH, BF, and NRJ further developed the protocol. JU, MM, and PK applied for funding. BF and JU set up the trial database. BF, MKC, JKT, TKM, and JU collected the study data and carried out the intervention. VS performed the statistical analysis and prepared the tables. JU and PK drafted the paper. All authors contributed to and approved the final paper."],"id":"S03-099.94-018333","identifier":[{"id":"85089827219","type":"scopus"},{"id":"32800792","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-4db5e5e5-269d-43b4-a27f-df529d6d48e4","ku-79ff954f-d241-4e3b-915d-bdbc62b4e967","sdu-bd5eeff5-49ba-4303-b9d2-f40d97679eb3"],"keyword":["Blood lipids","Blood pressure","Breast cancer rehabilitation","Faculty of Science","Fitness","Soccer","VO max","VO2max"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-54F8FFC8E8BA73DEFD1833E4010A4C72"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["University of Southern Denmark"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Nutrition, Exercise and Sports, Center for Holdspil og Sundhed","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Nutrition, Exercise and Sports, Movement and Neuroscience"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"fo","key":"university of the faroe islands","loc":["Faroe Islands","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic"],"name":"University of the Faroe Islands","used":1},{"affno":"5","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Clinical Epidemiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"gb","key":"university of exeter","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Exeter","used":1},{"affno":"7","country":"cn","key":"shanghai university of sport","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Shanghai University of Sport","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jacob","key":"uthjacobpau","last":"Uth","name":"Uth, Jacob","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Bjørn","key":"fristrupbjørnpau","last":"Fristrup","locid":"26bb6c78-4799-4562-81b7-fd714de59b59","name":"Fristrup, Bjørn","orcid":"0000-0002-8146-5170","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Victor","key":"sørensenvictorpau","last":"Sørensen","name":"Sørensen, Victor","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Eva Wulff","key":"helgeevawulffpau","last":"Helge","locid":"9bcf2b77-54e8-41d5-b356-d93a8350899b","name":"Helge, Eva Wulff","orcid":"0000-0002-4351-0137","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Maja Kjærgaard","key":"christensenmajakjærgaardpau","last":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Maja Kjærgaard","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Julie Boye","key":"kjærgaardjulieboyepau","last":"Kjærgaard","name":"Kjærgaard, Julie Boye","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Trine Kjeldgaard","key":"møllertrinekjeldgaardpau","last":"Møller","locid":"82f50dde-cd8e-4756-8d80-66c3a7b981e6","name":"Møller, Trine Kjeldgaard","orcid":"0000-0002-8337-9575","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Magni","key":"mohrmagnipau","last":"Mohr","locid":"37cc2c61-38d7-4481-a0a6-5dc859d17113","name":"Mohr, Magni","orcid":"0000-0002-1749-8533","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Jørn Wulff","key":"helgejørnwulffpau","last":"Helge","locid":"a5560afa-4f31-4278-8ae9-0c49fb0027c1","name":"Helge, Jørn Wulff","orcid":"0000-0001-9724-5423","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","first":"Niklas Rye","key":"jørgensenniklasryepau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"3dc68a95-e26c-4b82-a4f0-7548313f8045","name":"Jørgensen, Niklas Rye","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 5","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mikael","key":"rørthmikaelpau","last":"Rørth","locid":"f279398e-9773-4d96-be8a-832d3de47825","name":"Rørth, Mikael","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Eva Soelberg","key":"vadstrupevasoelbergpau","last":"Vadstrup","name":"Vadstrup, Eva Soelberg","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 6 7","corresponding":null,"first":"Peter","key":"krustruppeterpau","last":"Krustrup","locid":"466150ff-704b-461f-8004-692635d9c82b","name":"Krustrup, Peter","orcid":"0000-0002-1461-9838","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["32800792"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.pcad.2020.08.002","issn":["00330620","00330701"],"issn_org":["00330620"],"issue":"6","journal":"Progress in Cardiovascular Diseases","pages":"792-799","publisher":"WB  Saunders","publisher_org":"W.B. Saunders","status":"p","title":"Progress in Cardiovascular Diseases","volume":"63"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85089827219"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-4db5e5e5-269d-43b4-a27f-df529d6d48e4","ku-79ff954f-d241-4e3b-915d-bdbc62b4e967","sdu-bd5eeff5-49ba-4303-b9d2-f40d97679eb3"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Exercise intensity and cardiovascular health outcomes after 12 months of football fitness training in women treated for stage I-III breast cancer : Results from the football fitness After Breast Cancer (ABC) randomized controlled trial","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-099.94-018333","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Importance: Neurofilament light protein (NfL) is elevated in cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) of a number of neurological conditions compared with healthy controls (HC) and is a candidate biomarker for neuroaxonal damage. The influence of age and sex is largely unknown, and levels across neurological disorders have not been compared systematically to date. Objectives: To assess the associations of age, sex, and diagnosis with NfL in CSF (cNfL) and to evaluate its potential in discriminating clinically similar conditions. Data Sources: PubMed was searched for studies published between January 1, 2006, and January 1, 2016, reporting cNfL levels (using the search terms neurofilament light and cerebrospinal fluid) in neurological or psychiatric conditions and/or in HC. Study Selection: Studies reporting NfL levels measured in lumbar CSF using a commercially available immunoassay, as well as age and sex. Data Extraction and Synthesis: Individual-level data were requested from study authors. Generalized linear mixed-effects models were used to estimate the fixed effects of age, sex, and diagnosis on log-transformed NfL levels, with cohort of origin modeled as a random intercept. Main Outcome and Measure: The cNfL levels adjusted for age and sex across diagnoses. Results: Data were collected for 10 059 individuals (mean [SD] age, 59.7 [18.8] years; 54.1% female). Thirty-five diagnoses were identified, including inflammatory diseases of the central nervous system (n = 2795), dementias and predementia stages (n = 4284), parkinsonian disorders (n = 984), and HC (n = 1332). The cNfL was elevated compared with HC in a majority of neurological conditions studied. Highest levels were observed in cognitively impaired HIV-positive individuals (iHIV), amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, frontotemporal dementia (FTD), and Huntington disease. In 33.3% of diagnoses, including HC, multiple sclerosis, Alzheimer disease (AD), and Parkinson disease (PD), cNfL was higher in men than women. The cNfL increased with age in HC and a majority of neurological conditions, although the association was strongest in HC. The cNfL overlapped in most clinically similar diagnoses except for FTD and iHIV, which segregated from other dementias, and PD, which segregated from atypical parkinsonian syndromes. Conclusions and Relevance: These data support the use of cNfL as a biomarker of neuroaxonal damage and indicate that age-specific and sex-specific (and in some cases disease-specific) reference values may be needed. The cNfL has potential to assist the differentiation of FTD from AD and PD from atypical parkinsonian syndromes.","author_count":50,"contributors":["Bridel, Claire","van Wieringen, Wessel N","Zetterberg, Henrik","Tijms, Betty M","Teunissen, Charlotte E","Alvarez-Cermeño, José C","Andreasson, Ulf","Axelsson, Markus","Bäckström, David C","Bartos, Ales","Bjerke, Maria","Blennow, Kaj","Boxer, Adam","Brundin, Lou","Burman, Joachim","Christensen, Tove","Fialová, Lenká","Forsgren, Lars","Frederiksen, Jette L","Gisslén, Magnus","Gray, Elizabeth","Gunnarsson, Martin","Hall, Sara","Hansson, Oskar","Herbert, Megan K","Jakobsson, Joel","Jessen-Krut, Jan","Janelidze, Shorena","Johannsson, Gudmundur","Jonsson, Michael","Kappos, Ludwig","Khademi, Mohsen","Khalil, Michael","Kuhle, Jens","Landén, Mikael","Leinonen, Ville","Logroscino, Giancarlo","Lu, Ching-Hua","Lycke, Jan","Magdalinou, Nadia K","Malaspina, Andrea","Mattsson, Niklas","Meeter, Lieke H","Mehta, Sanjay R","Modvig, Signe","Olsson, Tomas","Paterson, Ross W","Pérez-Santiago, Josué","Romme Christensen, Jeppe","Sellebjerg, Finn"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-543644"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corpau":["and the NFL Group"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"diag